s trying to  run it and make sure it's efficient and  she raised some excellent points i do  think defense counsel has had it out for  the judge and has intentionally tried to  make things far more difficult so i  think the judge had a right to say all  that she said and during that little  clip i'm curious about their explanation  that whether or not that comes later and  what they do say kirk  how does a rebuttal case work and what  do you expect the prosecution to focus  on  well in a rebuttal there are two things  you want to achieve you want to attack  your adversary's strongest points and  then you want to reinforce your  strongest points  here the defense's strongest points are  the points they tried to make was that  uh nicholas  is not  responsible or not blameworthy enough to  be placed  to death because of his um  mental state and his prolonged history  of the emotional disturbance  um so they would want to rebut that by  stating that that still doesn't mean  that he's not blameworthy and further  reinforce their points  just quite simply the heinous nature of  the crime and keep it short and move on  yeah september 27th we'll see how that  plays out but of course a hearing before  that  and in washington efforts to get  brittany greiner released from a russian  prison continue  as president joe biden meets with the  families of the wnba star and the family  of paul whelan another american being  detained in the country greiner was  arrested in february earlier this year  when she was caught carrying vape  cartridges containing cannabis oil at  the moscow airport grind was charged  with attempting to smuggle illegal  narcotics into russia under russian law  the trial continued despite the guilty  plea in which griner took the stand in  her own defense she testified that she  did not intentionally bring an illegal  substance into the country she was  sentenced to a shocking nine years in  prison in order to pay a 16 thousand  dollar fine the u.s has been i